<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guillaume Du Fay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1474">1474</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Dufay, Du Fayt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guillaume Du Fay was a composer and music theorist of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> music, variously described as French or Franco-Flemish, and considered the leading European composer of his time. Educated at Cambrai Cathedral, he worked across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, joining the papal choir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1428 and later serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy</a>. In 1436 he wrote the motet Nuper Rosarum Flores for the consecration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a>'s dome for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>. After settling in Cambrai in 1458 he turned to cyclic masses based on cantus firmus, among them the Missa L'Homme armé, and influenced younger musicians including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-ockeghem">Johannes Ockeghem</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guillaume-du-fay.jpg" alt="Guillaume Du Fay" width="330" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Missal of Paul Beye · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DufayBinchois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Du Fay was born on 5 August 1397, probably in Beersel near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, the illegitimate child of an unknown priest and a woman named Marie Du Fayt, according to evidence in his will. She moved with her son to Cambrai, where a relative was a canon of the cathedral. He was listed as a choirboy from 1409 to 1412, and in 1411 he was given his own copy of Villedieu's Doctrinale Puerorum, highly unusual for one so young. By June 1414 he held a benefice as chaplain at St. Géry. He probably went to the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a> later that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1420 he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at Rimini and Pesaro for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">Malatesta family</a>. By 1426 he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in the service of Cardinal Louis Aleman, and by 1428 he was ordained priest. He then joined the papal choir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under Martin V and Eugene IV, and in 1434 became maistre de chappelle in Savoy to Duke Amédée VIII. In 1436 he composed Nuper rosarum flores for the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Santa Maria del Fiore</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He formed a long connection with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este family</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>.</p><p>He arrived in Cambrai by December 1439 and stayed through the 1440s, serving the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and revising the cathedral's liturgical music with Nicolas Grenon. He went to Savoy again in 1452, composing one of four Lamentationes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">Fall of Constantinople</a> in 1453 and the mass Se la face ay pale. On his return in 1458 he became a canon. He was visited by Busnois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-ockeghem">Ockeghem</a>, Tinctoris and Loyset Compère, and probably wrote the mass on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé</a> tune.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Du Fay died on 27 November 1474, after an illness of several weeks. He had asked that his motet Ave regina celorum be sung at his deathbed, but there was no time, and his Requiem, now lost, was performed at his funeral. He was buried in the chapel of St. Étienne in Cambrai cathedral. His tombstone was lost after the cathedral's destruction in the French Revolution, was found in 1859 covering a well, and is now in the Palais des Beaux Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>. Seven complete masses, 22 motets and 87 chansons by him survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guillaume Du Fay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He may be the name of a tavern, the Maison L'Homme Armé, near <b>Guillaume Du Fay</b>'s rooms in Cambrai.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li></ul></section>
